  "series": "BASH QUOTING & EXPANSION",
  "date": "07/09/2026",
  "slides": [
    {
      "page_title": "The Shell Reads a Line",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Fixed Order",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "brace",
              "tilde",
              "param + cmd + arith",
              "word split",
              "glob",
              "quote removal"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Tokenizing runs first, quote removal runs last.",
            "Redirections are set up after expansion.",
            "Then `execve` gets a finished argument vector."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Split After Expand"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Splitting and globbing happen AFTER expansion.",
            "Shell never re-parses a value for quotes.",
            "But it does split it and glob it."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "x='a b'; f $x      # 2 args: a, b",
            "x='a;b'; f $x      # 1 arg: a;b",
            "# the ; is data, not a separator"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Braces Are Textual"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`{a,b}c` gives `ac bc` — filesystem never consulted.",
            "`{1..3}.txt` names files that may not exist.",
            "`{1..$n}` fails: braces run before `$n` is known.",
            "Use `seq` or a C-style `for` loop instead."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Tilde Forms"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Form",
            "Becomes"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`~`",
              "`$HOME`"
            ],
            [
              "`~user`",
              "user's home"
            ],
            [
              "`~+`",
              "`$PWD`"
            ],
            [
              "`~-`",
              "`$OLDPWD`"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Only unquoted, only at word start.",
            "Also after `:` inside an assignment."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. The Substitutions"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`$(cmd)`: subshell, stdout, ALL trailing newlines stripped.",
            "`$(( ))`: integer math, bare names auto-dereferenced.",
            "So `$((x+1))` and `$(($x+1))` both work.",
            "`<(cmd)` is a path like `/dev/fd/63`, not text."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "diff <(sort a) <(sort b)   # two fake files",
            "> $file    # unquoted: ambiguous redirect",
            "x=$y       # assignment: no quotes needed"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Memorise One Sentence",
          "text": "The shell expands, then splits, then globs, then strips quotes. Every gotcha in this handbook falls out of that."
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "echo 'it'\\''s'   # -> it's",
            "# 'it'  +  \\'  +  's'  concatenated"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "\"$HOME\"/bin/'my app'   # ONE word",
            "$'\\t'                  # a real tab",
            "$'\\x41' $'\\u00e9'      # A and e-acute"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Single Quotes",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Literal and total: nothing expands.",
            "No escapes work at all inside.",
            "A single quote cannot appear inside, by any means."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "IFS=: read -r a b c <<< \"$line\"",
            "# prefix applies to this read only",
            "# no save/restore cleanup needed"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "The Three Quotes",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "7. Backslash & Joining"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Outside quotes it escapes any one next character.",
            "`a\\ b` is a single word `a b`.",
            "Adjacent pieces join with no separator."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "set -x                    # trace after expansion",
            "printf '[%s]\\n' $var      # count the arguments"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Double Quotes"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Live characters: `$`, backtick, `\\`, and `!`.",
            "Everything else is literal text.",
            "Backslash escapes only `$` ` \" \\ and newline.",
            "`\"\\n\"` is backslash-n, not a newline."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Dollar-Quoting"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`$'...'` is ANSI-C quoting: `\\n` `\\t` `\\x41` `\\u00e9` interpreted.",
            "Use `$'\\t'` to put a tab into `IFS` or a delimiter.",
            "`$\"...\"` is locale translation quoting — rare.",
            "With no catalog it silently acts like `\"...\"`."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "Double \"...\"",
                "lines": [
                  "expands vars: YES",
                  "blocks splitting: YES",
                  "literal `$`: only via `\\$`"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "Single '...'",
                "lines": [
                  "expands vars: NO",
                  "blocks splitting: YES",
                  "literal `$`: YES"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Quote",
            "Expands",
            "Blocks split"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "double",
              "YES",
              "YES"
            ],
            [
              "single",
              "NO",
              "YES"
            ],
            [
              "backslash",
              "NO",
              "YES (per char)"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. No Nesting"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Inside `'...'` a `\"` is just a character.",
            "Inside `\"...\"` a `'` is just a character.",
            "Globs are literal in ANY quoting.",
            "`\"*.txt\"` matches a file actually named `*.txt`."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Which Quote To Reach For",
          "text": "Double when you want expansion. Single for fixed literal text — regexes, awk programs, sed scripts. Backslash for one stray metacharacter."
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Deferred",
          "items": [
            "`\"$@\"` is a documented special case.",
            "Not a normal double-quoted string.",
            "Full treatment in the arrays chapter."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "echo a   b        # prints: a b",
            "# parser collapsed the spaces as tokens",
            "# different mechanism from word splitting"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Word Splitting and IFS",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. What Actually Splits",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Only results of unquoted parameter expansion.",
            "Plus command substitution and arithmetic expansion.",
            "Never literal text you typed yourself."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. IFS Basics"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`IFS` = Internal Field Separator.",
            "Default is space, tab, newline.",
            "`IFS=` (empty) disables splitting for that scope."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Whitespace Is Special"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "IFS",
            "Value",
            "Fields"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "default",
              "`'  a   b  '`",
              "2"
            ],
            [
              "`:`",
              "`a::b`",
              "3 (one empty)"
            ],
            [
              "empty",
              "anything",
              "1"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Whitespace runs collapse; edges are ignored.",
            "Non-whitespace separators never collapse.",
            "That empty `$PATH` entry means `.` for this reason."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Reading Lines"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "while IFS= read -r line; do",
            "  printf '%s\\n' \"$line\"",
            "done < file        # the only correct loop"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`IFS=` keeps leading and trailing whitespace.",
            "`-r` is mandatory: keeps backslashes intact.",
            "Without `-r`, backslash continues the line."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Why is `for x in $(cat file)` a bug?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "It splits on every whitespace run.",
            "Filenames with spaces shatter into pieces.",
            "Glob characters in the file then expand."
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "for f in $(cat list); do rm \"$f\"; done",
            "# a line 'my notes.txt' deletes two files"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Splitting On Purpose"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`arr=($string)` is the standard split idiom.",
            "Unquoted deliberately, with a chosen `IFS`.",
            "Splitting runs before globbing — a `*` field explodes.",
            "`set -f` disables globbing; `set +f` restores it."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "matrix",
          "spec": {
            "rows": [
              [
                "$x: zero args",
                "\"$x\": one empty arg"
              ]
            ],
            "highlight": [],
            "caption": "x='' then f $x vs f \"$x\""
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Null Field Rule",
          "text": "Unquoted `$x` on an empty value produces ZERO arguments. Quoted `\"$x\"` produces exactly one empty argument. Commands that count arguments notice."
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`*` also matches the empty string.",
            "Globs are NOT regexes.",
            "Glob `*` = any characters; regex `*` = repeat previous atom."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Globbing / Pathname Expansion",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. The Operators",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Glob",
            "Matches"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`*`",
              "any string, never `/`"
            ],
            [
              "`?`",
              "exactly one char"
            ],
            [
              "`[abc]`",
              "one of a b c"
            ],
            [
              "`[a-z]`",
              "range"
            ],
            [
              "`[!0-9]`",
              "negated class"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Classes And Dots"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`[[:digit:]]` `[[:alpha:]]` `[[:space:]]` inside brackets.",
            "Locale-aware, safer than raw `[a-z]` ranges.",
            "Leading dots never match `*`.",
            "Hidden files need `.*` or `shopt -s dotglob`."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. shopt Switches"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Option",
            "Effect"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`nullglob`",
              "no match -> nothing"
            ],
            [
              "`failglob`",
              "no match -> error"
            ],
            [
              "`globstar`",
              "`**` recursive"
            ],
            [
              "`extglob`",
              "`?() *() +() @() !()`"
            ],
            [
              "`nocaseglob`",
              "case-insensitive"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Without `nullglob`, `for f in *.txt` runs once on the literal `*.txt`.",
            "`failglob` suits scripts that must not proceed silently.",
            "`**/*.log` follows symlinked dirs in older Bash."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "shopt -s extglob",
            "rm !(keep.txt)      # all except keep.txt",
            "shopt -s globstar",
            "wc -l **/*.log      # recursive descent"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. The Shell Expands, Not ls"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flowchart",
          "spec": {
            "title": "ls *.txt",
            "rows": [
              [
                "n0"
              ],
              [
                "b0",
                "b1"
              ],
              [
                "c0_0",
                "c0_1"
              ],
              [
                "c1_0",
                "c1_1"
              ]
            ],
            "nodes": {
              "n0": {
                "text": "ls *.txt",
                "shape": "diamond"
              },
              "b0": {
                "text": "shell",
                "shape": "box"
              },
              "b1": {
                "text": "ls",
                "shape": "box"
              },
              "c0_0": {
                "text": "matches names",
                "shape": "box"
              },
              "c0_1": {
                "text": "builds argv",
                "shape": "box"
              },
              "c1_0": {
                "text": "sees a b c",
                "shape": "box"
              },
              "c1_1": {
                "text": "never sees `*`",
                "shape": "box"
              }
            },
            "edges": [
              {
                "from": "n0",
                "to": "b0"
              },
              {
                "from": "n0",
                "to": "b1"
              },
              {
                "from": "b0",
                "to": "c0_0"
              },
              {
                "from": "b0",
                "to": "c0_1"
              },
              {
                "from": "b1",
                "to": "c1_0"
              },
              {
                "from": "b1",
                "to": "c1_1"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "ARG_MAX",
          "text": "A glob matching 100k files can blow the argument limit: 'Argument list too long'. Use `find -exec ... +` or `xargs` instead."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Quoting Kills Globs"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A glob result is never split further — no quotes needed.",
            "Names with spaces survive `for f in *` intact.",
            "`\"*.txt\"` and `'*.txt'` are literal strings."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Parameter Expansion I: Defaults",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "find . -name '*.txt'   # find matches, not shell",
            "rm -- *                # guard leading dashes",
            "rm ./*                 # same guard, other way"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "No colon = only UNSET triggers the default.",
            "An empty variable stays empty.",
            "Same switch applies across all these operators."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Globs Inside Tests",
          "items": [
            "`[[ $f == *.txt ]]` glob-matches.",
            "Right side must be UNQUOTED to be a pattern.",
            "Quote it and you get a literal comparison."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Braces Disambiguate",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "${var}name    # value of var, then 'name'",
            "$varname      # value of varname (empty!)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Default Operators"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Form",
            "Does"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`${v:-d}`",
              "d if unset or empty"
            ],
            [
              "`${v:=d}`",
              "d and assign back"
            ],
            [
              "`${v:?msg}`",
              "error and exit"
            ],
            [
              "`${v:+alt}`",
              "alt only if set"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`:-` does not assign; `:=` does.",
            "`:=` cannot be used on positional parameters.",
            "`:?` prints to stderr, exits a non-interactive shell.",
            "`:+` expands to nothing otherwise."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. The Colon Switch"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "var is",
            "`${v:-d}`",
            "`${v-d}`"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "unset",
              "d",
              "d"
            ],
            [
              "empty",
              "d",
              "empty"
            ],
            [
              "`x`",
              "x",
              "x"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            ": \"${API_KEY:?API_KEY is required}\"",
            "cmd ${verbose:+--verbose}   # conditional flag",
            "${var:?}    # empty msg still errors, fine as a guard"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "for n in \"${!APP_@}\"; do",
            "  printf '%s=%s\\n' \"$n\" \"${!n}\"",
            "done          # enumerate config vars"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Safe with newlines in filenames.",
            "No fork means no process-creation cost.",
            "A loop of `basename` pays milliseconds per iteration."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Still Quote The Whole Thing",
          "text": "Write `\"${var:-default}\"`. The default text is itself subject to word splitting, so an unquoted expansion leaks the same old bug."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Parameter Expansion II: Trimming",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Length & Indirection"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`${#var}` is the character length of the value.",
            "`${#arr[@]}` is the element count of an array.",
            "`${!var}` is indirect: var='HOME' yields `$HOME`.",
            "`${!prefix*}` and `${!prefix@}` list names by prefix."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Positionals & Specials"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Token",
            "Means"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`$1`..`$9`",
              "arguments"
            ],
            [
              "`${10}`",
              "braces required"
            ],
            [
              "`$#` `$?`",
              "count, exit status"
            ],
            [
              "`$$` `$!`",
              "PID, last bg PID"
            ],
            [
              "`$0` `$-`",
              "script name, flags"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Trim From The Ends",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Form",
            "Removes"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`${v#p}`",
              "shortest prefix"
            ],
            [
              "`${v##p}`",
              "longest prefix"
            ],
            [
              "`${v%p}`",
              "shortest suffix"
            ],
            [
              "`${v%%p}`",
              "longest suffix"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "On a US keyboard `#` sits left of `%`.",
            "`#` trims from the left — that is the hook.",
            "Patterns are GLOBS, not regexes.",
            "They must stay unquoted to act as patterns."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Path Surgery"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "${path##*/}    # basename, no fork",
            "${path%/*}     # dirname, no fork",
            "${file%.*}     # strip extension",
            "${file##*.}    # get extension"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Search & Replace"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Form",
            "Replaces"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`${v/o/n}`",
              "first match"
            ],
            [
              "`${v//o/n}`",
              "all matches"
            ],
            [
              "`${v/#o/n}`",
              "anchored at start"
            ],
            [
              "`${v/%o/n}`",
              "anchored at end"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "${var//[[:space:]]/}   # delete = replace with nothing",
            "${var:2}               # from index 2 to the end",
            "${var:0:4}             # first four characters",
            "${var: -3}             # last three (mind the space)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Case modification needs Bash 4+.",
            "`${v@Q}` is the correct way to build a string for `eval` or `ssh`.",
            "`${v@A}` emits a reusable declaration."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Element-Wise On Arrays"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "files=(a.txt b.txt c.txt)",
            "printf '%s\\n' \"${files[@]%.txt}\"",
            "# a b c  -- applied to every element"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Arrays: Indexed and Associative",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Case And @ Transforms"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Form",
            "Result"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`${v^}` `${v^^}`",
              "upper first / all"
            ],
            [
              "`${v,}` `${v,,}`",
              "lower first / all"
            ],
            [
              "`${v@Q}`",
              "shell-safe quoted"
            ],
            [
              "`${v@U}` `${v@E}`",
              "upper / unescape"
            ],
            [
              "`${v@A}`",
              "assignment stmt"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "/var/log/app.log",
              "`##*/` -> app.log",
              "`%.*` -> app"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Zero Forks",
          "text": "Every operator on this page runs inside the shell. Replacing `basename`/`dirname`/`sed` in a hot loop removes one process spawn per iteration."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Declare And Index",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "arr=(a b c)        # or: declare -a arr",
            "echo \"${arr[0]}\"   # a -- zero indexed",
            "echo $arr[0]       # WRONG: $arr then '[0]'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Elements are words, subject to the normal expansions.",
            "Braces around a subscript are mandatory.",
            "Bare `$arr` means `${arr[0]}`, not the whole array.",
            "That silent truncation is a very common bug."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. [@] Versus [*]"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "\"${arr[@]}\"",
                "lines": [
                  "one word per element",
                  "boundaries preserved",
                  "right 95% of the time"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "\"${arr[*]}\"",
                "lines": [
                  "one joined word",
                  "joined by first IFS char",
                  "only when you want a string"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Unquoted `${arr[@]}` re-splits and re-globs each element.",
            "That destroys any element containing spaces.",
            "Quote it, always, unless splitting is the goal."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Count, Indices, Append"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Form",
            "Gives"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`${#arr[@]}`",
              "element count"
            ],
            [
              "`${#arr[0]}`",
              "length of element 0"
            ],
            [
              "`${!arr[@]}`",
              "the indices"
            ],
            [
              "`arr+=(new)`",
              "append"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Indexed arrays go sparse after `unset arr[1]`.",
            "So iterate indices, not `0..count-1`.",
            "`arr+=new` without parens appends to element zero."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "\"${arr[@]:1:2}\"   # two elements from index 1",
            "\"${@:2}\"          # all args after the first"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Loading From Files"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "mapfile -d '' files < <(find . -print0)",
            "mapfile -t lines < file   # -t strips newlines"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "\"$@\" vs $*: Passing Arguments",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Associative Arrays"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "declare -A map          # FIRST, or all keys -> 0",
            "map[host]=example.com",
            "echo \"${map[host]}\"",
            "for k in \"${!map[@]}\"; do :; done"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Without `declare -A` the subscript is evaluated as arithmetic.",
            "Keys via `\"${!map[@]}\"`, values via `\"${map[@]}\"`.",
            "Iteration order is hash order, not insertion order."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "The Killer Use Case",
          "text": "Arrays are the only correct way to build a command with variable arguments: `cmd=(rsync -a); [[ $dry ]] && cmd+=(--dry-run); \"${cmd[@]}\" src dst`. No `eval`, no quoting damage."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. The Two Forms",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Form",
            "Result"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`\"$@\"`",
              "one word per arg"
            ],
            [
              "`\"$*\"`",
              "one joined word"
            ],
            [
              "`$@` / `$*`",
              "split + glob (WRONG)"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`\"$@\"` expands to `\"$1\" \"$2\" ...`.",
            "`\"$*\"` joins by first IFS char (space).",
            "Unquoted forms are identical and both wrong."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Empty Case"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Zero Arguments",
          "text": "With zero args, `\"$@\"` expands to zero words. `\"$*\"` expands to one empty word. This is why `\"$@\"` is safe in `cmd \"$@\"`."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Comparison Row"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Args: `a`, `b c`",
            "Count"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`\"$@\"`",
              "2 arguments"
            ],
            [
              "`\"$*\"`",
              "1 argument (`a b c`)"
            ],
            [
              "`$@`",
              "3 arguments"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Forwarding"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "exec real-command \"$@\"",
            "# preserves argument boundaries exactly",
            "# never write: cmd $*"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Function `$1` shadows script `$1`.",
            "`$0` remains the script name inside functions.",
            "`shift` drops `$1` and renumbers."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Slicing Positionals"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "\"${@:2}\"       # all args after first",
            "\"${@:$#}\"      # the last argument",
            "args=(\"$@\")    # copy preserving boundaries"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Prefer `[[ ... ]]` in Bash.",
            "No word splitting or globbing on operands.",
            "Quote RIGHT side of `==` unless globbing."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Gotchas: Where Missing Quotes Bite",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Legitimate `\"$*\"`"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Joining for a log message.",
            "Building a delimited string with custom IFS.",
            "`(IFS=,; echo \"$*\")` prints a CSV row."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "The Golden Rule",
          "text": "Never write `cmd $*` in a wrapper script. It is the single most common way scripts break on paths containing spaces."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. The `rm -rf` Trap",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "rm -rf $dir/       # if dir='', becomes rm -rf /",
            "rm -rf \"${dir:?dir required}\"/",
            "# guard with :? to prevent empty expansion"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Test Syntax"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "[ $x = y ]      # ERROR if x is empty",
            "[ \"$x\" = y ]   # correct",
            "[ \"x$x\" = xy ] # legacy workaround"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Output Primitives"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Command",
            "Issue"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`echo $var`",
              "collapses whitespace, globs"
            ],
            [
              "`echo -n`",
              "not portable"
            ],
            [
              "`printf '%s\\n'`",
              "reliable, portable"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Filenames"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Filenames may contain spaces, newlines, quotes.",
            "Only `/` and NUL are forbidden.",
            "Use `find -print0` / `xargs -0` / `mapfile -d ''`.",
            "Leading dashes: `rm -- \"$f\"` or `rm \"./$f\"`."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Command Substitution"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "x=$(cat f)          # loses trailing newlines",
            "x=$(cat f; printf x)",
            "x=${x%x}            # restore them"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`$(...)` nests cleanly; backticks are legacy.",
            "Unquoted redirection: 'ambiguous redirect' error.",
            "`eval` re-parses everything — avoid it."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Exit Status"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`export VAR=$(cmd)` masks cmd's exit status.",
            "Split assignment from export when checking failures.",
            "`set -e` does not fire in command substitutions.",
            "Do not rely on `set -e` as a safety net."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Numeric Contexts",
          "text": "Inside `$(( ))` and array subscripts, splitting does not happen. Quotes are optional there."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Rules, Tools, and the Checklist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. The Seven Rules",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "1. Quote every expansion by default.",
            "2. Exceptions: `[[ ]]`, `(( ))`, assignments, globs.",
            "3. Use arrays, never strings, for lists.",
            "4. `\"$@\"` to forward; `\"$*\"` only for joining.",
            "5. `-print0` / `-0` / `-d ''` for filenames.",
            "6. `printf '%s\\n'` instead of `echo`.",
            "7. Parameter expansion over forking tools."
          ],
          "style": "number"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Script Preamble"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "set -euo pipefail",
            "IFS=$'\\n\\t'",
            "# knowing set -e has holes is key"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Debugging Tools"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Tool",
            "Use"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`shellcheck`",
              "static analysis, SC2086"
            ],
            [
              "`set -x`",
              "trace after expansion"
            ],
            [
              "`declare -p`",
              "show type and quoting"
            ],
            [
              "`printf '<%s>\\n'`",
              "count arguments"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Portability"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Arrays, `${var,,}`, `${var@Q}` are Bash-only.",
            "Absent from POSIX `sh`.",
            "`#!/bin/sh` must use `\"$@\"`, `case`, `set --`."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Closing Summary"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "The One Sentence",
          "text": "The shell expands, then splits, then globs, then strips quotes. Quotes turn off splitting and globbing. Arrays preserve boundaries that a single string cannot."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Final Takeaway"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "If a variable is not inside double quotes, you have promised the shell it contains no spaces, no globs, and no surprises — and filenames break that promise every day."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
